Alert Manager
-----------------

If your product-catalog deplloyment has no available replicas ,prometheus can detect the problem and fire an alert .Alert manager receives that alert and routes a notification your configured receiver

Step 1: Verify the Alert Manager
-----------------------------------
>> kubectl get pods -n monitoring | grep alertmanager
>> kubectl get svc -n monitoring | grep alertmanager

Open AlertManger UI
___________________

>> kubectl port-forward -n monitoring svc/prometheus-stack-prom-alertmanager
9093:9093



Step 2: Configure the Alert manager
------------------------------------
Understand teh alert manager interface


Explore these sections:
- Alerts: Currently firing alerts.
- Silences: Temporarily suppress selected alerts.
- Status: Alertmanager configuration and runtime information.


We will here configure the AlertManager with the Slack so When every there is a alert the slack will get notified by the AlertManager


The approach will be:
1.Create a Slack Incoming webhook
2.Store the Webhook URL in kubernetes secret
3.Configure teh alertmanager to use that secret
4.Update the helm release while preserving your existiong values


Step 1: Create a Slack Incoming Webhook

1.Search for the Slack API in the Browser and click on the Slack API page 
2.Click on the Create new app
3.Inside this click on the Blank app
4.Give app a name and connect with the workspace you have created in the slack 
Here for example : Kubernetes Alertmanger
5.Continues continues and create
6.Search for the Incomming Webhooks
7.Activate the Incoming Webhooks  by turning it on which was earlier off
8.next click on the add webhook 
   which will take us to the new page wher you select to which channel it has to send the message 
   here it is: all-kubernetes-alertmanager
9. And allow 
10.Copy the created webhook and save it



Next we will connect the Slack webhook to the AlertManager Using a Kubernetes Secret:
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Create a Secret file in the appropriate directory

>> vim slack-webhook-secret.yaml

apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret

metadata:
  name: slack-webhook
  namespace: monitoring

type: Opaque

stringData:
  slack_api_url: "PASTE_YOUR_SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL_HERE"

Apply this file: 
>> kubectl apply -f slack-webhook-secret.yaml

Ok next we'll configure the existing kube-prometheus-stack helm release to ues your Slack secret

We'll make two change: 
1.Mount Slack-WebHook into AlertManager
2.Configure Alertmanager to send notificatoin to your slack channel



Back Up your Current Helm values:
----------------------------------

Run: 
helm get values prometheus-stack -n monitoring -o yaml \
  > prometheus-stack-values.yaml

This create a local copy of your current custom helm configuration


alertManager:
  # Alertmanager main configuration 
  # Defines how alerts are grouped and routed and delivered

  config: 
    # Global settings applid to alll alert notifications
    global: 
      # wait up to 5 minutes befoer considering an alert resolved
      resolved_timeout: 5m
      
    # Defines where the alert to be sent
    route: 
      # send alerts to this receiver by default
      receiver: slack-notification
      
      # Group alerts sharing the same alert name and namespace
      # Example: Mulitple PodDown alerts in ecommerce
      # can  be combined into one notificatiions
      
      group_by: 
        - alertname
        - namespace

      # Wait 30 seconds after the first alert before sending
      # the initial notification. This allows related alerts
      # to arrive and be grouped together.
   
      group_wait: 30s
      
      # Wait 5 minutes before sending notifications for
      # newly added alerts in an existing group.
      group_interval: 5m

      # Send repeat notifications every 4 hours
      # if an alert remains firing.
      repeat_interval: 4h
  
    # Defines the notification receivers.
    # A receiver is the destination where alerts are delivered.
    receivers:
      - name: slack-notifications

        # Slack notification integration.
        slack_configs:

          # Read the Slack webhook URL from a mounted Secret file.
          # This avoids hardcoding the sensitive webhook URL.
          - api_url_file: /etc/alertmanager/secrets/slack-webhook/slack_api_url

            # Slack channel where notifications will be delivered.
            channel: "#kubernetes-alerts"

            # Send a notification when an alert is resolved.
            # This means you receive both firing and resolved alerts.
            send_resolved: true

            # Template used for the Slack notification title.
            title: '{{ template "slack.default.title" . }}'

            # Template used for the Slack notification message.
            text: '{{ template "slack.default.text" . }}'
      - name: "null"

    alertmanagerSpec:

    # Mount the existing Kubernetes Secret into Alertmanager.
    # The Secret must exist in the monitoring namespace.
    secrets:
      - slack-webhook

    # Run one Alertmanager replica.
    replicas: 1

    # CPU and memory limits and requests for Alertmanager.
    resources:
      limits:
        cpu: 500m
        memory: 512Mi
      requests:
        cpu: 50m
        memory: 128Mi

    # Retain Alertmanager notification data for 120 hours (5 days).
    retention: 120h

    # Persistent storage configuration for Alertmanager.
    storage:
      volumeClaimTemplate:
        spec:
          # Allow one node to mount this volume for read/write.
          accessModes:
            - ReadWriteOnce

          resources:
            requests:
              # Request a 5 GiB PersistentVolume.
              storage: 5Gi

          # Use Minikube's standard StorageClass.
          storageClassName: standard

  # Enable Alertmanager installation.
  enabled: true



